\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{A positive invariant density can destroy reflection positivity}
\date{2026-11-17}

\p{Return to the independent uniform signs #{(x,y)} with
reflection exchanging them in \ref{fdip-00XG}. Their
unweighted law is reflection positive on functions of #{x}.
The density
##{w(x,y)=1-\frac{xy}{2}}
is positive, reflection invariant and normalized, since
#{\mathbb E(xy)=0}. Nevertheless the positive-half function
#{h(x)=x} has weighted reflection square
##{\mathbb E\bigl(w(x,y)(\Theta h)(x,y)h(x,y)\bigr)
 =\mathbb E\bigl((1-xy/2)xy\bigr)=-\frac12.}
Thus even positive reflection-invariant reweighting need
not preserve reflection positivity. The pointwise-fixed
reflection in \ref{fdip-00XZ} supplies a stronger hypothesis
than invariance of the density.}
